% primitives.elpi: what elpi-debug needs to know about the terms and the
% unification variables of a run and does not find in Elpi's core, as a
% small interface of primitives that Elpi could offer as builtins (the
% wish list, ../ELPI-WISHLIST.md), written here in Elpi as STAND-INS until
% Elpi has them.  The other files of the interpreter go through this
% interface only: switching to the real builtins touches this file only.
%
% THE INTERFACE, section by section (§N: the item of ../ELPI-WISHLIST.md)
%
% 1. [vars-of T Vs] (§1): Vs are the unification variables occurring in
%    T, each once, in the order of their first occurrence (left to right,
%    depth first), under binders and inside applications of builtins.  A
%    variable applied to arguments (X a b) is given by its head X.  It
%    binds nothing.
%
% 2. [uuid X N] (§2): N identifies the unification variable X for the
%    whole run: two variables have different numbers, and a variable keeps
%    its number at every event and across backtracking.  X must be a
%    variable (it fails otherwise); a variable bound to another one has
%    the number of that one (Elpi dereferences it).  [uuid-name X S]:
%    "X<N>", the name elpi-debug shows for X.  [show-term T S]: T printed
%    on one line, its unification variables named X<uuid>, its
%    eigenvariables by the names in force ([eigen E Name], lprolog.elpi).
%
% 3. The trail (§3).  [trail-mark G M]: M marks the current point of the
%    trail, before the goal G runs (G is for the stand-in only: the real
%    builtin needs no argument).  [bindings-since M Bs]: Bs are the
%    bindings made since M and still in force, one [binding N V P] per
%    variable that existed at M and is bound now: N its uuid, V its value
%    now, P the pruning Elpi made when it bound it: [not-pruned];
%    [pruned-arguments U K N], it was replaced by the variable of uuid U
%    applied to K of its N arguments; [pruned-scope Es], by a variable
%    that may no longer depend on the eigenvariables Es.  The variables
%    created after M are not listed: they are parts of the values.  (The
%    order of Bs: the stand-in gives the order of vars-of at the mark; the
%    real builtin may give the order of the trail.)
%
% 4. The shape of a term (§9).  [shape-of T S]: T taken apart, one level:
%    a variable, a leaf, a head and its arguments, or a binder;
%    [conjuncts G Gs]: the goals of the conjunction G; [binder-body F X B]:
%    the body of the λ-term F for X.
%
% 5. The scope of a variable (§11).  [can-depend V E]: the variable V may
%    be assigned a term mentioning the eigenvariable E.
%    [visible-eigens V Es]: the eigenvariables in scope that V may depend
%    on, oldest first.
%
% The other stand-ins of elpi-debug, outside this file: the CHR hooks
% (chr-hooks.elpi, §4-§6), the bridges as static rules (program.elpi,
% §7), the unwinding of retry (stepper.elpi, "Unwinding", §10), and in the
% driver (../bin): the parsed program given to the API (§8) and the CHR
% rules rewritten (chr_in_view.ml, §4-§6).
%
% THE STAND-INS (below), and their limits
%
% - vars-of walks the term ([shape-of]), a ground subterm skipped whole
%   (the native test ground_term).  Elpi has no builtin to take apart an
%   application of a builtin (X is Y + 1 is one node), so the walk does
%   not see the variables there; nor those of a conjunction of more than
%   16 goals.  Hence reflect's option -closed: the debugger cannot
%   abstract the variables of a rule itself, reflect gives each rule
%   closed (compiled.elpi, [compile-rules]).  The walk gets the head of an
%   applied variable with the builtin var; on a variable that Elpi keeps
%   in a compact form (G x, x the binder above, printed X0^1 by Elpi) that
%   head is a new variable, bound in place of the old one: from then on
%   the variable shows under a second number.
% - uuid reads the number Elpi's printer gives the variable.  The printer
%   keeps, for the whole run, a table from Elpi's internal identity of a
%   variable to a number given at its first print ("X12", "X12^1" for a
%   variable created under one binder): it is the one place where Elpi
%   lets a program see that identity.  A table kept by the debugger
%   cannot do it: a safe refuses a term with variables ("expected: closed
%   term"), local clauses do not outlive the goal that adds them, and
%   same_var cannot tell a variable from one bound to it (both dereference
%   to the same).  The stand-in is wrong inside a copy (std.findall copies
%   its goal and prints with a table of its own, from X0): the debugger
%   names no variable inside std.findall.  show-term edits the printer's
%   text: the depths dropped ("X12^1" is X12), the line breaks of long
%   terms replaced by spaces, and each eigenvariable c<level> that has a
%   name in force replaced by it, with a regular expression.
% - trail-mark cannot see the trail: it takes a snapshot of the variables
%   in reach of G, and bindings-since compares them with what they are
%   now: O(variables in reach) at the mark and again at bindings-since.
%   What is in reach of a goal: the variables of the goal, and those of
%   the hypothetical clauses in scope (a clause added by => shares its
%   variables with the goal that added it; the frames above give no other
%   access).  Missed: a variable reached only through a suspended goal of
%   the constraint store (CHR), or inside an application of a builtin
%   (vars-of above).
% - shape-of tells the connectives by one pattern each, and a conjunction
%   by one pattern per number of goals, up to 16 ([conjuncts]); an
%   application of a builtin written in OCaml (var X, X is Y + 1, print X)
%   is a leaf; a λ-term is told by its printed text ([is-lambda]).
% - can-depend tries the binding V = E and undoes it (Elpi refuses it when
%   E is younger than V): visible-eigens costs one such try per
%   eigenvariable in scope.
%
% Nothing here runs on the fast path (no event shown or recorded), except
% [conjuncts], which the compilation of a clause body uses
% (interpreter.elpi, compiled.elpi).


% ===== 1. The variables of a term ==============================================

% [vars-of T Vs]: the unification variables of T (stand-in: the walk of
% [shape-of], which does not look into applications of builtins).
pred vars-of i:any, o:list any.
vars-of T Vs :- vars-acc T [] Rev, std.rev Rev Vs.

% [vars-acc T Acc Acc1]: Acc1 is Acc and, most recent first, the variables
% of T not in Acc.
% A ground subterm has none: the native test ground_term skips it whole
% (most of the data of a goal, such as a list of numbers) instead of an
% interpreted walk of several goals per node.
pred vars-acc i:any, i:list any, o:list any.
vars-acc T Acc Acc :- ground_term T, !.
vars-acc T Acc Acc1 :- shape-of T S, shape-vars S Acc Acc1.

% [shape-vars S Acc Acc1]: [vars-acc] for a term of shape S.
pred shape-vars i:shape, i:list any, o:list any.
shape-vars (flexible H) Acc Acc :- std.exists Acc (same_var H), !.
shape-vars (flexible H) Acc [H|Acc].
shape-vars leaf Acc Acc.
shape-vars (node _ Ts) Acc Acc1 :- vars-acc-all Ts Acc Acc1.
shape-vars (binder F) Acc Acc1 :- pi x\ sigma B\ binder-body F x B, vars-acc B Acc Acc1.

% [vars-acc-all Ts Acc Acc1]: [vars-acc] for each of the terms Ts in turn.
pred vars-acc-all i:list any, i:list any, o:list any.
vars-acc-all [] Acc Acc.
vars-acc-all [T|Ts] Acc Acc2 :- vars-acc T Acc Acc1, vars-acc-all Ts Acc1 Acc2.

% ===== 2. The identity of a variable, and printing ==================================

% [uuid X N]: the number of the variable X for the whole run (stand-in:
% the number of Elpi's printer, "X12^1" for 12).
pred uuid i:any, o:int.
uuid X N :- var X H _, term_to_string H S, printer-number S N.

% [printer-number S N]: S is the printer's name of a variable, "X<N>"
% followed by its depth ("^1") if any.
func printer-number string -> int.
printer-number S N :- rex.replace "^X\\([0-9]+\\).*$" "\\1" S Digits, N is string_to_int Digits.

% [uuid-name X S]: "X<uuid>", the name elpi-debug shows for the variable X.
func uuid-name any -> string.
uuid-name X S :- uuid X N, !, uuid-label N S.
uuid-name X S :- term-string X S.       % (not a variable: printed)

% [uuid-label N S]: "X<N>", the name of the variable of uuid N.
func uuid-label int -> string.
uuid-label N S :- S is "X" ^ {std.any->string N}.

% [show-term T S]: T printed on one line, its unification variables named
% X<uuid>, its eigenvariables by their source names (the [eigen] clauses
% in force).  The real builtin would be Elpi's printer itself, naming each
% variable by its identity (the printer has the line, commented out:
% "X" ^ string_of_int (uvar_id r)) and each eigenvariable by a name the
% caller gives, on one line.
func show-term any -> string.
show-term T S :- term-string T S1, names Es, rename-eigens Es 0 S1 S.

% [term-string T S]: T printed on one line, each of its variables named
% X<uuid> (stand-in: the printer's text, whose numbers are the uuids,
% without the depth of each variable, "X12^1" printed X12).
func term-string any -> string.
term-string T S :- term_to_string T S0, plain-text S0 S.

% [plain-text S0 S]: the printer's text S0 on one line (a line break and
% the indentation after it become a space), without the depths of its
% variables ("X12^1" becomes X12).  Most texts have neither: one test for
% both is cheaper than the two replacements (each regular expression is
% compiled at each call, about 10 µs).
func plain-text string -> string.
plain-text S0 S :- rex.match "[^\n^]*[\n^]" S0, !,
  rex.replace "\n *" " " S0 S1, rex.replace "\\bX\\([0-9]+\\)\\^[0-9]+" "X\\1" S1 S.
plain-text S S.

% [rename-eigens Es L S0 S]: S is the text S0 in which each eigenvariable
% of Es that has a name in force, printed c<level> by Elpi, is replaced by
% its name; Es are the eigenvariables in scope from level L on, the
% oldest first (as [names] gives them, whatever its documentation says:
% its code is list_init 0 depth).
func rename-eigens list any, int, string -> string.
rename-eigens [] _ S S.
rename-eigens [E|Es] L S0 S :- rename-eigen E L S0 S1, L1 is L + 1, rename-eigens Es L1 S1 S.

% [rename-eigen E L S0 S]: the eigenvariable E, of level L, renamed in S0.
func rename-eigen any, int, string -> string.
rename-eigen E L S0 S :- eigen E Name, !,
  Rex is "\\bc" ^ {std.any->string L} ^ "\\b", rex.replace Rex Name S0 S.
rename-eigen _ _ S S.

% ===== 3. The trail ========================================================================

% A mark of the trail (stand-in: a snapshot of the variables in reach).
data trail-mark.
symb snapshot list var-snapshot -> trail-mark.

% A variable at the mark: itself, its uuid, and the eigenvariables it may
% depend on (to tell a pruning of its scope).
data var-snapshot.
symb var-snapshot any -> int -> list any -> var-snapshot.

% A binding made since a mark: the uuid of the variable, its value now,
% and the pruning Elpi made when it bound it.
data binding.
symb binding int -> any -> pruning -> binding.

data pruning.
symb not-pruned pruning.
symb pruned-arguments int -> int -> int -> pruning.  % the variable of uuid U gets K of the N arguments
symb pruned-scope list any -> pruning.               % may no longer depend on these eigenvariables

% [trail-mark G M]: a mark of the trail now, before the goal G runs
% (stand-in: a snapshot of the variables that the goals run for G may
% bind, [in-reach]; the real builtin would not look at G).
pred trail-mark i:(pred), o:trail-mark.
trail-mark G (snapshot Ss) :- in-reach G R, vars-of R Vs, std.map Vs snapshot-of Ss.

% [snapshot-of V S]: the variable V as the snapshot keeps it.
pred snapshot-of i:any, o:var-snapshot.
snapshot-of V (var-snapshot V N Vis) :- uuid V N, visible-eigens V Vis.

% [in-reach G R]: R holds what the goals run for G may bind: G and the
% hypothetical clauses in scope ([hyp], interpreter.elpi), which share
% their variables with the goals that added them.
pred in-reach i:(pred), o:any.
in-reach G (pr G Vs) :- hyps-vars [] Vs.

% [hyps-vars Acc Vs]: Acc and the variables of the hypothetical clauses in
% scope, gathered one clause at a time (std.findall would copy them).
pred hyps-vars i:list any, o:list any.
hyps-vars Acc Vs :- hyp _ _ C, vars-of C CVs, new-vars CVs Acc New, not (New = []), !,
  std.append Acc New Acc1, hyps-vars Acc1 Vs.
hyps-vars Acc Acc.

% [new-vars Vs Known New]: the variables of Vs not in Known.
pred new-vars i:list any, i:list any, o:list any.
new-vars [] _ [].
new-vars [V|Vs] Known New :- std.exists Known (same_var V), !, new-vars Vs Known New.
new-vars [V|Vs] Known [V|New] :- new-vars Vs Known New.

% [bindings-since M Bs]: the bindings made since the mark M, still in
% force (stand-in: each variable of the snapshot that is no longer
% itself).
pred bindings-since i:trail-mark, o:list binding.
bindings-since (snapshot Ss) Bs :- bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs.

% [bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs]: the bindings of the variables of Ss bound now.
pred bindings-of-snapshots i:list var-snapshot, o:list binding.
bindings-of-snapshots [] [].
bindings-of-snapshots [var-snapshot V N Vis | Ss] [binding N V P | Bs] :- bound-since V N, !,
  pruning-of V Vis P,
  bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs.
bindings-of-snapshots [_ | Ss] Bs :- bindings-of-snapshots Ss Bs.

% [bound-since V N]: the variable of uuid N, V, is bound: it is now a
% term, or (headed by) another variable.
pred bound-since i:any, i:int.
bound-since V N :- not (uuid V N).

% [pruning-of V Vis P]: what Elpi pruned when it bound V: V is now
% x1..xn\ H a1..ak with k < n (H does not get all the arguments), or V
% is now a variable (applied or not) that may depend on fewer
% eigenvariables than Vis, those V might depend on at the mark.
pred pruning-of i:any, i:list any, o:pruning.
pruning-of V _ (pruned-arguments U K N) :- lambda-prefix V 0 N K U, N > 0, K < N, !.
pruning-of V Vis (pruned-scope Lost) :- var V, visible-eigens V Now, lost-eigens Vis Now Lost, not (Lost = []), !.
pruning-of _ _ not-pruned.

% [lambda-prefix T N0 N K U]: T is N-N0 binders around H a1..ak, H the
% variable of uuid U, k = K; the test is made under the binders, where
% the body lives.  (The builtin var is asked for the arguments only: asked
% for the head and the arguments together, it binds the variable to a new
% one, which would then show under another number.)
pred lambda-prefix i:any, i:int, o:int, o:int, o:int.
lambda-prefix T N0 N K U :- shape-of T S, S = binder F, !, N1 is N0 + 1,
  pi x\ sigma B\ binder-body F x B, lambda-prefix B N1 N K U.
lambda-prefix T N N K U :- var T _ Args, std.length Args K, uuid T U.

% [lost-eigens Before Now Lost]: the eigenvariables of Before not in Now.
pred lost-eigens i:list any, i:list any, o:list any.
lost-eigens [] _ [].
lost-eigens [E|Es] Now Lost :- std.exists Now (same_term E), !, lost-eigens Es Now Lost.
lost-eigens [E|Es] Now [E|Lost] :- lost-eigens Es Now Lost.

% ===== 4. The shape of a term =============================================================
%
% Elpi has no builtin to take a λ-term, a connective or an application of
% a builtin apart (constant and name fail on them); [shape-of] does it
% once for all the walks of the debugger (vars-of above, lprolog.elpi,
% the explanation).

data shape.
symb flexible any -> shape.        % a logic variable (its head, for an applied one)
symb leaf shape.                   % a primitive datum, [], !, or a builtin application
symb node any -> list any -> shape.  % a head (constant, eigenvariable, list-cell
                                   % or connective-node) and its arguments
symb binder any -> shape.          % pi, sigma or a λ-term, with its body as a λ-term

% the heads given to list cells and to connectives
symb list-cell any.
symb connective-node any.

% [shape-of T S]: the shape of T.  (Call it with S unknown: its clauses are
% guarded by cuts.)
pred shape-of i:any, o:shape.
shape-of T (flexible H) :- var T H _, !.
shape-of T leaf :- is_cdata T _, !.
shape-of (pi x\ F x) (binder F) :- !.
shape-of (sigma x\ F x) (binder F) :- !.
shape-of G (node connective-node Gs) :- conjuncts G Gs, !.
shape-of (A => B) (node connective-node [A, B]) :- !.
shape-of (A :- B) (node connective-node [A, B]) :- !.
shape-of (A = B) (node connective-node [A, B]) :- !.
shape-of (pattern_match A B) (node connective-node [A, B]) :- !.
shape-of (findall_solutions A B) (node connective-node [A, B]) :- !.
shape-of (declare_constraint G K) (node connective-node [G, K]) :- !.
shape-of ! leaf :- !.
shape-of T (node H Args) :- constant T H Args, !.
shape-of T (node H Args) :- name T H Args, !.
shape-of [] leaf :- !.
shape-of T (node list-cell [X, L1]) :- T = [X|L], !, std.unsafe-cast L L1.
shape-of T (binder T) :- is-lambda T, !.
shape-of _ leaf.          % an application of a builtin (var X ...): not looked into

% [is-lambda T]: T is a λ-term.  Elpi has no builtin for it, and unifying
% T with x\ F x would eta-expand anything; a λ-term prints as "cN \ ...".
pred is-lambda i:any.
is-lambda T :- term_to_string T S, rex.match "^c[0-9]+ \\\\" S.

% [binder-body F X B]: B is the body of the λ-term F for the eigenvariable X.
pred binder-body i:any, i:any, o:any.
:untyped
binder-body F X (F X).

% [conjuncts G Gs]: G is a conjunction of the goals Gs.  Elpi represents
% (A, B, C) as one n-ary node and has no builtin to take it apart, so each
% arity needs its own pattern; up to 16 goals at the same level.
% (Building a conjunction needs no stand-in: (G, C) nested runs the same,
% [mk-conj], interpreter.elpi.)
pred conjuncts i:(pred), o:list (pred).
conjuncts (A1,A2) [A1,A2].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3) [A1,A2,A3].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4) [A1,A2,A3,A4].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13,A14) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13,A14].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13,A14,A15) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13,A14,A15].
conjuncts (A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13,A14,A15,A16) [A1,A2,A3,A4,A5,A6,A7,A8,A9,A10,A11,A12,A13,A14,A15,A16].

% ===== 5. The scope of a variable ============================================================

% [can-depend V E]: the logic variable V may be assigned a term mentioning
% the eigenvariable E (stand-in: the binding tried and undone).
pred can-depend i:any, i:any.
can-depend V E :- not (not (V = E)).

% [visible-eigens V Es]: the eigenvariables in scope that V may depend on.
pred visible-eigens i:any, o:list any.
visible-eigens V Es :- names All, keep-visible V All Es.

% [keep-visible V Es Vs]: the eigenvariables of Es that V may depend on.
pred keep-visible i:any, i:list any, o:list any.
keep-visible _ [] [].
keep-visible V [E|Es] [E|Vs] :- can-depend V E, !, keep-visible V Es Vs.
keep-visible V [_|Es] Vs :- keep-visible V Es Vs.
